 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The construction of the TSP order is performed once per training session. The per-iteration cost is dominated by the rendering and gradient computation for the active set, similar to standard 3DGS, but without the overhead of random shuffling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for spatial blocks and the view permutation. The out-of-core streaming capability allows handling scenes with over one billion primitives by loading blocks as needed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves optimization stability and convergence for large-scale scenes by reducing gradient variation between consecutive steps [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of the TSP order. May be less robust to scenes with highly discontinuous camera paths compared to random shuffling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Large-scale scenes where optimization stability is critical and camera paths exhibit trajectory continuity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with random or highly discontinuous camera trajectories where trajectory continuity assumptions do not hold [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** The TSP ordering is computed once during initialization. The training loop processes each view exactly once per epoch, similar to standard 3DGS, but with the overhead of block-wise frustum culling and Morton-code based spatial partitioning [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for spatial blocks and Morton codes. The out-of-core streaming capability allows handling scenes with over one billion primitives by loading only active blocks into GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Improves optimization stability and convergence for large-scale scenes by reducing gradient variation between consecutive steps [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Reduces gradient drift; mitigates stale optimizer state through masked updates; enables scalable training via out-of-core optimization [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of TSP order; relies on visibility-induced sparsity for effectiveness [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Large-scale scenes where standard random shuffling leads to unstable convergence or slow training [S1, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** Small scenes where random shuffling is sufficient and the overhead of TSP ordering is unjustified [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-TideGS was introduced in the paper "TideGS: Scalable Training of Over One Billion 3D Gaussian Splatting Primitives via Out-of-Core Optimization" presented at ASPLOS '26 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the standard 3DGS optimization framework but addresses scalability and stability issues in large-scale scenes by combining out-of-core streaming with trajectory-aware view ordering [S1, unofficial]. The method draws intuition from ordering-aware SGD analyses, particularly the work of Mohtashami et al. (2022) [S1, unofficial].
+TideGS was introduced in the paper "TideGS: Scalable Training of Over One Billion 3D Gaussian Splatting Primitives via Out-of-Core Optimization" [S1, unofficial]. It departs from standard randomized view order to address scalability and stability issues in large-scale 3D Gaussian Splatting training. The method leverages empirical properties of visibility-induced sparsity and trajectory continuity to improve optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
